[{:db/id 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9aa267d9-9214-57a8-8b47-8d7c0275750b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9aa267d9-9214-57a8-8b47-8d7c0275750b" :question/id #uuid "9aa267d9-9214-57a8-8b47-8d7c0275750b" :question/math-academy-id "q-175392" :question/problem "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36>0,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36>0$\n\nLet's first rearrange our inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36 & >0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & >36 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & >1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that this represents an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes $3$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/96/9658f04618246f6c35ab0d22c7998accb5708791cdfc756cd276654b7e8fb18a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0cc62668-b9db-5986-a4dc-ae1caed64a15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0cc62668-b9db-5986-a4dc-ae1caed64a15" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cc62668-b9db-5986-a4dc-ae1caed64a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b9713476-1578-522a-9962-a958111f1a0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5704fa4b-54ad-5908-b5f1-f9b8c86cf289" "answer-ed9d83e8-3eed-527f-b289-9b339765d1b3" "answer-b9713476-1578-522a-9962-a958111f1a0c" "answer-3b6e0f3c-e66d-5670-9f6b-a1d8e8d3ec26" "answer-34edf70b-723f-5261-ad29-e021c72a2740"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5704fa4b-54ad-5908-b5f1-f9b8c86cf289" :answer/id #uuid "5704fa4b-54ad-5908-b5f1-f9b8c86cf289" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1b/1b73fdc417e59f631f821989a2a12f34cc7abc1d7c220cad667c07d3f5556c1c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed9d83e8-3eed-527f-b289-9b339765d1b3" :answer/id #uuid "ed9d83e8-3eed-527f-b289-9b339765d1b3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/57/57281b013e998ca0450f7ef5ff7e203495e89b577040b2a2fb17c161896e62ea.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9713476-1578-522a-9962-a958111f1a0c" :answer/id #uuid "b9713476-1578-522a-9962-a958111f1a0c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/96/9658f04618246f6c35ab0d22c7998accb5708791cdfc756cd276654b7e8fb18a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b6e0f3c-e66d-5670-9f6b-a1d8e8d3ec26" :answer/id #uuid "3b6e0f3c-e66d-5670-9f6b-a1d8e8d3ec26" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b85a63b7a77b057d2fd81e4890f9c2ee35b5ae6f1bb3cedfe3203ed68f2d4080.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34edf70b-723f-5261-ad29-e021c72a2740" :answer/id #uuid "34edf70b-723f-5261-ad29-e021c72a2740" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c8/c8d307b09344ecba6c9412edd9e89dc6a7b723117e85ac8e2995af31ac52bc36.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" :question/id #uuid "34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162827" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.16$ | $0.09$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.44$ | $0.21$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.09.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.3\\cdot 0.3=0.09.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2) & =0.16\\ne 0.3\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-287256f7-b082-5a2a-854b-414b7b8a37a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-287256f7-b082-5a2a-854b-414b7b8a37a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "287256f7-b082-5a2a-854b-414b7b8a37a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f1ed2e1-55cd-57e9-aa50-68794e5c6eb6" "answer-162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" "answer-0777157f-29ea-5e24-9e8e-6b9a24e51747" "answer-ee2dac8c-ea1a-540c-93ab-6f8d5b85be8c" "answer-da46d0be-42c1-57a1-ab4a-e8a9512a5cad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f1ed2e1-55cd-57e9-aa50-68794e5c6eb6" :answer/id #uuid "0f1ed2e1-55cd-57e9-aa50-68794e5c6eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/id #uuid "162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0777157f-29ea-5e24-9e8e-6b9a24e51747" :answer/id #uuid "0777157f-29ea-5e24-9e8e-6b9a24e51747" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee2dac8c-ea1a-540c-93ab-6f8d5b85be8c" :answer/id #uuid "ee2dac8c-ea1a-540c-93ab-6f8d5b85be8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da46d0be-42c1-57a1-ab4a-e8a9512a5cad" :answer/id #uuid "da46d0be-42c1-57a1-ab4a-e8a9512a5cad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4b5e1083-38a8-5ebe-a155-656595d8bb85" :activity/id #uuid "4b5e1083-38a8-5ebe-a155-656595d8bb85" :activity/title "Quiz 5" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-225f3922-71d0-5828-b8ff-8fffbfb93314" "step-dcc43919-02cb-5986-afb0-91015014e6f6" "step-d7953f7e-ce6e-54dc-8567-32347c44dc0b" "step-f7fcb20d-433e-5fe0-9143-ce6e7eaa5c1a" "step-8b8b53b4-c35a-5ca7-a8ea-c47534397dd5" "step-cd274b10-c0ca-520e-a49b-be984f15746a" "step-f541f3e5-d2a5-58a4-bb6b-ba161c260278"] :activity/first-step "step-225f3922-71d0-5828-b8ff-8fffbfb93314" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-225f3922-71d0-5828-b8ff-8fffbfb93314" :step/id #uuid "225f3922-71d0-5828-b8ff-8fffbfb93314" :step/content 17592186342241 :step/next "step-dcc43919-02cb-5986-afb0-91015014e6f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dcc43919-02cb-5986-afb0-91015014e6f6" :step/id #uuid "dcc43919-02cb-5986-afb0-91015014e6f6" :step/content 17592186398576 :step/next "step-d7953f7e-ce6e-54dc-8567-32347c44dc0b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d7953f7e-ce6e-54dc-8567-32347c44dc0b" :step/id #uuid "d7953f7e-ce6e-54dc-8567-32347c44dc0b" :step/content 17592186429817 :step/next "step-f7fcb20d-433e-5fe0-9143-ce6e7eaa5c1a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7fcb20d-433e-5fe0-9143-ce6e7eaa5c1a" :step/id #uuid "f7fcb20d-433e-5fe0-9143-ce6e7eaa5c1a" :step/content 17592186115884 :step/next "step-8b8b53b4-c35a-5ca7-a8ea-c47534397dd5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8b8b53b4-c35a-5ca7-a8ea-c47534397dd5" :step/id #uuid "8b8b53b4-c35a-5ca7-a8ea-c47534397dd5" :step/content "question-34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" :step/next "step-cd274b10-c0ca-520e-a49b-be984f15746a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd274b10-c0ca-520e-a49b-be984f15746a" :step/id #uuid "cd274b10-c0ca-520e-a49b-be984f15746a" :step/content 17592186287749 :step/next "step-f541f3e5-d2a5-58a4-bb6b-ba161c260278" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f541f3e5-d2a5-58a4-bb6b-ba161c260278" :step/id #uuid "f541f3e5-d2a5-58a4-bb6b-ba161c260278" :step/content 17592186429818 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161704 :question/problem "Workers of a small company decided to elect a new union representative. There are $50$ workers, and $60\\%$ of this worker population will vote for candidate $A.$ Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n![](images/c2/c28a8c33f7c9beeb8132564b044489ff480065b047327bda1e8eae8a84218ca2.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50.$ Find $P(X_2 = 0 | X_1 = 0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N = 50,$ and $p = 0.6$ represents the proportion of the population that will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The number of members that will vote for candidate $A$ equals\n\n$$\nNp = 50(0.6) = 30.\n$$\n\n- The number of members that will *not* vote for candidate $A$ equals\n\n$$\nN(1 - p) = 50(1 - 0.6) = 20.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P(X_2 = 0 | X_1 \n= 0),$ the probability that the second member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ given that the first member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ either.\n\nIf the first member does *not* vote for candidate $A,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $N - 1 = 50 - 1 = 49$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\qquad$ $N(1 - p)-1 = 19$ members remaining that will not vote for candidate $A.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 | X_1 = 0) = \\dfrac{N(1 - p)-1}{N-1} = \\dfrac{19}{49}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163344 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a company is about to elect a new union representative. We're interested in determining the proportion $p$ of a company's employees that will vote for \"candidate $A.$\"\n\nIf the company has $N = 50$ employees, and $30$ of them will vote for candidate $A,$ then the proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{30}{50} = 0.6.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $60\\%$ of the company's employees will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $N$ is the **population size**\n\n- $p$ is the **population proportion**\n\n- The number of employees that will vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nNp = 50\\cdot 0.6 = 30.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, our goal is to build foundations for estimating $p$ in cases where the population size $N$ is very large. Note that although the population size $N$ is not very large in this example, we can still use it to help develop the key ideas.\n\nWe start by modeling the response from each member of the population as a random variable $X_i,$ defined by\n\n![](images/76/76b2ae3dd8206bfdbe208d0e037bd1efa5614101917cf6f981df078cb3cdc8cf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50.$ \n\nCalculating the probability that a randomly selected member of the population will vote for candidate $A$ is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{i}=1) & =\\frac{\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A}{\\text{population size}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np}{N} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{50} \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to note that the $X_i$'s are *dependent!* For example,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\neq P(X_2 = 1 ).\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that $P(X_2 = 1) = 0.6,$ as before. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{(\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A)-1}{\\text{population size}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\cdot 0.6-1}{50-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-1}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{49} \\\\  & ≈0.592 \\\\  & \\ne P({X}_{2}=1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's interpret this result. We know that the first member will vote for candidate $A.$ So, to compute the conditional probability that the second member will *also* vote for candidate $A,$ we consider a reduced population with $N-1 = 49$ members, of which only $30-1 = 29$ will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nCalculating $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 0)$ works similarly, as we'll see in the following example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163346 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population of size $N=1\\,000$ of which the proportion $p=0.5$ has a particular characteristic. We denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a801fdb0d37e38011713448593b185b440445d6a57b0ff42e00c21e66401f6bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,1\\,000.$\n\nTechnically speaking, as we saw previously, the $X_i$'s are *dependent*.\n\nThis time, however, since the population size $N = 1\\,000$ is large, we may *approximate* that the $X_i$'s are *independent*. \n\nTo see why, we first note that \n\n$$\nP(X_2=1) = \\dfrac{Np}{N} = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000\\cdot 0.5-1}{1\\,000-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{500-1}{999} \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{999} \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we've shown that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\approx P(X_2 = 1),\n$$\n\nsuggesting that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are (approximately) independent." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163345 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32bc52b6268f6c5eeb9a6f07fff58c308871cc650541c16a4c595faf81e833df.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nIt can be shown that the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables *provided that* the population size $N$ is *significantly larger* than the sample size $n$ (which we denote as $N \\gg n$). In practice, this typically means that the sample size should be no larger than $5\\%$ of the population size.\n\nThe assumption that the population size is much larger than the sample size is true in many cases. For example, the U.S. voting population consists of hundreds of millions of people, yet a typical sample from this population might consist of only a few thousand voters. \n\nLet's now consider the following statistic:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i\n$$\n\nwhere $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$ \n\nSince the $X_i$'s take the value $1$ if the $i$th element has the characteristic or $0$ if the $i$th element doesn't have the characteristic, the statistic $X$ can be used to count the number of elements with the characteristic. In other words, $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic.\n\nWe wish to determine the sampling distribution of $X.$ Note the following:\n\n- Each $X_i$ can be modeled as a Bernoulli random variable, i.e., $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$\n\n- Since we're assuming that $N\\gg n,$ it follows that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are (approximately) *independent and identically distributed* (I.I.D) Bernoulli random variables.\n\n- It can be shown that a sum of $n$ I.I.D. Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p$ is a binomial random variable $B(n,p)$ (we'll prove this in a separate lesson). Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nWe summarize this process using the following flow chart.\n\n![](images/f5/f50d54432c86d4b41ed842218a70b93617457de3e518aa88cbe6ea28762f37ea.png)\n\nFinally, using the formulas for the mean and variance of a Binomial random variable, we have the following approximations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163347 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/46/4624ca7ee7660803ddc7165252c3725030b13ab255f56dc5d87e23090c2363bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N,$ then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i =  \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot np \\\\  & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot np(1-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance $\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163349 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of $\\widehat{\\,p}$  is called the **standard error** of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}\n$$\n\nIf $p$ is unknown, we can replace it with its estimate $\\widehat{\\,p}$ in the standard error formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nNote that we have written $\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$ to denote that the above expression is an estimate of $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] .$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162231 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8d08405b-e533-59a3-974d-02400c4f8447" :activity/id #uuid "8d08405b-e533-59a3-974d-02400c4f8447" :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049184 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-61d61754-7b4b-53fa-b355-e1e2744b21c1" "step-7c21cb12-f528-579b-9e03-4e7e70d8e424" "step-619efe09-3031-5ed9-849a-5c2f5bf2ac5a" "step-4fb77fcc-508c-53c1-9504-71147177444b" "step-aa2781e1-2aff-5e27-88ae-47fd0dd86c13"] :activity/first-step "step-61d61754-7b4b-53fa-b355-e1e2744b21c1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-61d61754-7b4b-53fa-b355-e1e2744b21c1" :step/id #uuid "61d61754-7b4b-53fa-b355-e1e2744b21c1" :step/content 17592186198618 :step/next "step-7c21cb12-f528-579b-9e03-4e7e70d8e424" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7c21cb12-f528-579b-9e03-4e7e70d8e424" :step/id #uuid "7c21cb12-f528-579b-9e03-4e7e70d8e424" :step/content 17592186405069 :step/next "step-619efe09-3031-5ed9-849a-5c2f5bf2ac5a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-619efe09-3031-5ed9-849a-5c2f5bf2ac5a" :step/id #uuid "619efe09-3031-5ed9-849a-5c2f5bf2ac5a" :step/content 17592186371981 :step/next "step-4fb77fcc-508c-53c1-9504-71147177444b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4fb77fcc-508c-53c1-9504-71147177444b" :step/id #uuid "4fb77fcc-508c-53c1-9504-71147177444b" :step/content 17592186281375 :step/next "step-aa2781e1-2aff-5e27-88ae-47fd0dd86c13" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-aa2781e1-2aff-5e27-88ae-47fd0dd86c13" :step/id #uuid "aa2781e1-2aff-5e27-88ae-47fd0dd86c13" :step/content 17592186281376 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128991 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$,