nswer-field/choices 17592186131542] [:db/retract 17592186131539 :answer-field/choices 17592186131543] [:db/retract 17592186131539 :answer-field/choices 17592186131544] [:db/retract 17592186134300 :question/answer-fields 17592186131839] {:db/id 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eff4218e-d396-5a2a-a2a2-2a662de74575" "question-ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" "question-a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133595 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/36/3693f5fe916109a80a0d7782f7d21781953a7f854cedf82568d353d7e9d23681.svg)\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133657 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](images/dd/dd8614491da535daffac48a2966a3f832f81a94f16f4dc1fea8a2bea7d24b61b.svg) \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133596 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/16/16f0578d54931024f488302f40489449cc5d8a76bed2dea13b85edd29c486dd2.svg)\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133659 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n![](images/52/52f781dc28ffc1d3af7a1c381f78fcc3982f195a9e8af406551a93f1c4f5823b.svg)\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326817 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+9\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-eff4218e-d396-5a2a-a2a2-2a662de74575" :question/id #uuid "eff4218e-d396-5a2a-a2a2-2a662de74575" :question/math-academy-id "q-81473" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-53237724-b18f-554e-b4b7-e3f73a8381db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-53237724-b18f-554e-b4b7-e3f73a8381db" :answer-field/id #uuid "53237724-b18f-554e-b4b7-e3f73a8381db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07028645-efe7-5074-be80-058ffbade705" "answer-6e2a905a-0b20-5d17-a56b-1c89e206592e" "answer-2eb48d44-3d17-50ac-a866-63b0e50f60cf" "answer-4e8768fa-dee4-512e-9646-4b4d01ba6296" "answer-f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07028645-efe7-5074-be80-058ffbade705" :answer/id #uuid "07028645-efe7-5074-be80-058ffbade705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2a905a-0b20-5d17-a56b-1c89e206592e" :answer/id #uuid "6e2a905a-0b20-5d17-a56b-1c89e206592e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eb48d44-3d17-50ac-a866-63b0e50f60cf" :answer/id #uuid "2eb48d44-3d17-50ac-a866-63b0e50f60cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e8768fa-dee4-512e-9646-4b4d01ba6296" :answer/id #uuid "4e8768fa-dee4-512e-9646-4b4d01ba6296" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e" :answer/id #uuid "f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" :question/id #uuid "ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40617" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1538404b-ed05-55f2-a776-3683d1df3c57"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1538404b-ed05-55f2-a776-3683d1df3c57" :answer-field/id #uuid "1538404b-ed05-55f2-a776-3683d1df3c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82d0e36d-d943-5853-8c22-a5f342e793d8" "answer-16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" "answer-6d27de48-288e-5ed9-9d7d-0d0eae6d2993" "answer-e241ba1f-eb80-5d8a-bbe0-22c86cdf6403" "answer-c405d893-f4de-5b73-b692-4d9ba63fe661"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82d0e36d-d943-5853-8c22-a5f342e793d8" :answer/id #uuid "82d0e36d-d943-5853-8c22-a5f342e793d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" :answer/id #uuid "16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d27de48-288e-5ed9-9d7d-0d0eae6d2993" :answer/id #uuid "6d27de48-288e-5ed9-9d7d-0d0eae6d2993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e241ba1f-eb80-5d8a-bbe0-22c86cdf6403" :answer/id #uuid "e241ba1f-eb80-5d8a-bbe0-22c86cdf6403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c405d893-f4de-5b73-b692-4d9ba63fe661" :answer/id #uuid "c405d893-f4de-5b73-b692-4d9ba63fe661" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405" :question/id #uuid "a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405" :question/math-academy-id "q-40618" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e1fe577e-eb1d-5087-b60c-da2397f3533b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1fe577e-eb1d-5087-b60c-da2397f3533b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fe577e-eb1d-5087-b60c-da2397f3533b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" "answer-e0b3a4d2-a564-5688-9d33-b1279a57f1d0" "answer-5e4a973a-58b0-5903-bddb-9f88ac25c8e6" "answer-3d2d0b6e-9b33-5b70-809d-a505e2ff9a95" "answer-fba0c9ac-4d4c-5112-acf7-decbe76a6bc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" :answer/id #uuid "91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0b3a4d2-a564-5688-9d33-b1279a57f1d0" :answer/id #uuid "e0b3a4d2-a564-5688-9d33-b1279a57f1d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e4a973a-58b0-5903-bddb-9f88ac25c8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5e4a973a-58b0-5903-bddb-9f88ac25c8e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d2d0b6e-9b33-5b70-809d-a505e2ff9a95" :answer/id #uuid "3d2d0b6e-9b33-5b70-809d-a505e2ff9a95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fba0c9ac-4d4c-5112-acf7-decbe76a6bc8" :answer/id #uuid "fba0c9ac-4d4c-5112-acf7-decbe76a6bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134250 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131539 :answer-field/correct "answer-b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6516927-d038-5c53-9ff0-064cf2766c4c" "answer-b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" "answer-f65d1b05-4896-5427-ac5b-938cfa80e269" "answer-af016e3c-6583-5076-8547-2da29a030814" "answer-b835f025-1455-54be-a739-0b1bfc31db5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6516927-d038-5c53-9ff0-064cf2766c4c" :answer/id #uuid "b6516927-d038-5c53-9ff0-064cf2766c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" :answer/id #uuid "b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f65d1b05-4896-5427-ac5b-938cfa80e269" :answer/id #uuid "f65d1b05-4896-5427-ac5b-938cfa80e269" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af016e3c-6583-5076-8547-2da29a030814" :answer/id #uuid "af016e3c-6583-5076-8547-2da29a030814" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b835f025-1455-54be-a739-0b1bfc31db5d" :answer/id #uuid "b835f025-1455-54be-a739-0b1bfc31db5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388674 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=3[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394200 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 4+14\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\text{ }\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351336 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351339 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+(-11)\\cdot (-4)+17\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 5+(-11)\\cdot 4+17\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186373278 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272067 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134260 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131599 :answer-field/correct 17592186131600 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134269 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{7}{3})}^{2}+{(2-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131653 :answer-field/correct 17592186131657 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326863 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186373318 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134300 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{v\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{w\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{2}{3}{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326859 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The vector $x$ can be written as $x={a}_{1}-{a}_{2}.$ So, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, the projection of $x$ onto $S$ is $x$ itself and the angle between $x$ and the subspace is equal to $0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" :question/id #uuid "887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40217" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33a073ec-2091-5bcd-b60b-ebba7bccb98d" "answer-c8e3caac-58c5-5c9f-b488-f943b767b28f" "answer-4cd33462-6550-5cd3-834f-318520fe8bc5" "answer-14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" "answer-0c04b137-6afd-5c93-bffd-c2ad52b0f5a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33a073ec-2091-5bcd-b60b-ebba7bccb98d" :answer/id #uuid "33a073ec-2091-5bcd-b60b-ebba7bccb98d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25.58}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8e3caac-58c5-5c9f-b488-f943b767b28f" :answer/id #uuid "c8e3caac-58c5-5c9f-b488-f943b767b28f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{52.58}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cd33462-6550-5cd3-834f-318520fe8bc5" :answer/id #uuid "4cd33462-6550-5cd3-834f-318520fe8bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55.82}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/id #uuid "14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{58.52}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c04b137-6afd-5c93-bffd-c2ad52b0f5a5" :answer/id #uuid "0c04b137-6afd-5c93-bffd-c2ad52b0f5a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{85.25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351474 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351473 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135088 :tutorial/content "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135087 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the formula for the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspaces $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1 \\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ given that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\perp \\mathbf{a}_2.$ \n\nWe start by writing $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S=\\text{proj}_S\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is in the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$. That is, $\\mathbf{x}_S=k_1\\mathbf{a}_1+k_2\\mathbf{a}_2\\in S\\,$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp.$\n\nFrom the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is orthogonal to $S,$ and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a}_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {a}_{1}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{1})=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {a}_{2}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{2})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})=x\\cdot {a}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=x\\cdot {a}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}} \\\\ {k}_{2}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain our formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134002 :activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186062257 :answer-field/choices 17592186062258] [:db/retract 17592186062257 :answer-field/choices 17592186062259] [:db/retract 17592186062257 :answer-field/choices 17592186062261] [:db/retract 17592186061194 :answer-field/choices 17592186061195] [:db/retract 17592186061194 :answer-field/choices 17592186061197] [:db/retract 17592186061194 :answer-field/choices 17592186061198] [:db/retract 17592186061194 :answer-field/choices 17592186061199] {:db/id 17592186064279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b1106a2d-af4e-5322-aea1-c8e92fa92e1a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064122 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{16y^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $16$ and $y^2$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{16{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4\\cdot y\\cdot y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot y\\cdot 4\\cdot y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4y\\cdot 4y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}} & =|4y| \\\\  & =|4|\\cdot |y| \\\\  & =4|y|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064305 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7244134b-92da-5194-8740-fd44c3d9b173" "question-fbd7fbcf-f71d-5f1d-91fa-fd513daaf842"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064141 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2c^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $36, a^2,$ and $c^2$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}{c}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6\\cdot 6\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot c\\cdot c} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6\\cdot a\\cdot c\\cdot 6\\cdot a\\cdot c} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6ac)\\cdot (6ac)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}} & =|6ac| \\\\  & =|6|\\cdot |ac| \\\\  & =6|ac|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064330 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ea1bc4b-df65-5c2e-8a1e-25b530d6cd97"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063968 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4x^2}{y^2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2,$ and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot x\\cdot 2\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{2x}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|2x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2|x|}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1106a2d-af4e-5322-aea1-c8e92fa92e1a" :question/id #uuid "b1106a2d-af4e-5322-aea1-c8e92fa92e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129414" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{{z}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{z}^{2}}=|z|.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afffccf8-b1b8-53bd-9ab1-fc9b40dfecf3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afffccf8-b1b8-53bd-9ab1-fc9b40dfecf3" :answer-field/id #uuid "afffccf8-b1b8-53bd-9ab1-fc9b40dfecf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3e87eba-1191-550d-93b0-4de6bc1bc641" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee296152-fc8a-54a9-9d6f-71bbfd30d366" "answer-12197615-b745-5519-9cc6-05632d3bf3bb" "answer-0e891d34-6c44-5f95-b17b-99dd8094812d" "answer-fef3c8c3-5a09-538a-8384-95b8300e46c8" "answer-b3e87eba-1191-550d-93b0-4de6bc1bc641"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee296152-fc8a-54a9-9d6f-71bbfd30d366" :answer/id #uuid "ee296152-fc8a-54